I’ll first inspect the pattern, metadata, and the frozen analysis guidance, then build a composition-aware phase inventory before fitting. I’ll keep the refinement reversible and verify that the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, and final GSAS-II project all agree.

The chemical context is Mn–W–O with Cu Kα reflection data and no declared product formula or phase count. I’m now mapping the experimental peak families and screening all accessible Mn/W/O COD records—including precursor polymorphs and plausible manganese tungstates—before any Rietveld refinement.

The peak inventory already resolves two dominant families: monoclinic WO₃ (the 23–25° triplet and 33–35° cluster) and rutile-type β‑MnO₂ (29.0°, 37.7°, 42.2°, 57.0°, 59.7°, 65.1°, 72.2°). MnWO₄ and other Mn–W–O/Mn-oxide polymorphs remain on the watch list; I’m now comparing alternate crystallographic records under the same baseline model so a poor source record is not mistaken for phase absence.

The first screening pass exposed an important source issue: a 1931 WO₃ record reproduces some peak positions but contains a 0.46 Å contact and imports with degraded symmetry, so it is physically rejected. I have normalized the viable records with the same symmetry/Uiso conventions used by the framework and am rerunning the comparison with stabilized scales and staged cell refinement.

The restrained baseline confirms both phase fractions near 71/29 wt%, but its unconstrained common width model tries to absorb phase-specific broadening (W→0, X and U at bounds). I’m rejecting that width solution and returning to the converged cells/scales/background. Next I’m testing size and strain from neutral values one phase at a time, as the distinct observed widths—about 0.17–0.19° for WO₃ versus 0.12–0.15° for MnO₂—require.

Under the same background, scales, displacement, cells, and width terms, the modern neutron WO₃ record (COD 2311041) improves Rwp from 7.72% to 6.36% over the older room-temperature model while converging to the same lattice. That is strong evidence that the residual was structural (chiefly oxygen coordinates), not a missing phase or extra nuisance effect. I’m completing the triclinic/orthorhombic challenges before refining coordinates.

The common polarization term is strongly supported (refined to about 0.86 without roughness). A low-angle roughness model further improves the fit for the triclinic candidate with physical interior values, whereas SH/L is unsupported and highly correlated with displacement, so SH/L is being pruned. I’m now challenging texture axes; any accepted March–Dollase term must move reproducibly away from 1 and improve multiple families.

The texture challenge is decisive only for β‑MnO₂: [110] March–Dollase refines to about 0.81 and improves several isolated families; every WO₃ axis returns to 1.0. A weak [001] WO₃ strain anisotropy is supported, while MnO₂ anisotropy is not replay-stable and is pruned. I also found the two-parameter roughness/polarization fit drifting along a strong correlation, so I’m reducing roughness to one identifiable parameter before any coordinate refinement.

The phase inventory is now converging on WO₃ plus rutile β‑MnO₂. I’m auditing the coordinate-refinement branch for chemically sensible bond geometry before accepting it, then I’ll re-challenge plausible minor phases and perform the final GSAS-II replay/export consistency check.

The unconstrained oxygen refinement lowers Rwp, but it moves WO₃ oxygen sites by about 0.19 Å RMS—well outside the defensible source-model mismatch range—so I’m rejecting that branch as overfit. The tungsten-only shifts remain modest (~0.05 Å RMS) and preserve sensible WO₆ distances; I’m now testing the single rutile oxygen coordinate separately and examining parameter correlations.

The watch-list replay found no support for Mn₃WO₆ or hausmannite and only sub-significance support for γ‑MnO₂. Two candidates require closer scrutiny: MnWO₄ (~0.24 wt%) and Mn₂O₃ (~0.63 wt%) each refined positive, but their Rwp gains are very small. I’m testing whether each is backed by multiple coherent peak families and whether either survives a simultaneous, replay-stable fit before deciding whether to report them or treat them as below reliable detection.

The common MnO₂ displacement parameter is strongly supported and physically bounded (Uiso ≈ 0.0181 Å²); adding it improves Rwp from 5.546% to 5.477% and brings the major-phase fractions close to the precursor stoichiometry. The two weak candidate scales persist, so I’m next using a physically bounded WO₃ oxygen-coordinate shrinkage test to see whether they are merely compensating for source-structure mismatch.

The physically bounded baseline is now replay-stable at Rwp 5.456%, with no frozen parameters: WO₃ 73.50 wt% and β‑MnO₂ 26.50 wt%. Both weak candidates still refine positive under that clean profile (MnWO₄ ≈0.23 wt%, Mn₂O₃ ≈0.59 wt%), so I’m testing them together; this is the decisive check for whether they compete for the same residual or represent distinct peak families.

A GSAS-II scripting side effect surfaced during the source-record comparison: adding a phase autosaved into the loaded baseline before the output path was switched. I caught it before finalization; I’m rebuilding the clean two-phase baseline from its uncontaminated precursor and updating the comparison scripts so every branch is isolated. No submitted files have been created yet.

The joint weak-phase fit is stable and noncompetitive: MnWO₄ remains ~0.27 wt% (5.7σ scale) and Mn₂O₃ ~0.62 wt% (7.2σ). The three Mn₂O₃ records are nearly indistinguishable at this abundance; I’m retaining the higher-symmetry cubic Ia‑3 bixbyite model and will flag that symmetry assignment as tentative. A fixed-profile roughness scan now favors a physical Suortti pair A=0.40, B≈0.253 (38% attenuation at 20°); I’m reconverging the full four-phase model with that pair fixed to avoid its strong scale correlation.

The selected model now passes the geometry screen for all four structures (no short contacts), has a strictly positive smooth background, no frozen parameters, and an independently recomputed Rwp of 5.420% (Rp 4.064%, GOF 2.016). The remaining mismatch is mainly low/mid-angle intensity—not a peak-position trend—which is why the sub‑1 wt% phases and the exact WO₃/Mn₂O₃ symmetries will be reported with explicit uncertainty. I’m moving to exact project/CIF/profile replay and export checks now.

The independent replay regenerated the full 8,301-point profile from atomic models—without using saved calculated intensities—and reproduced the submitted fit to numerical precision. It also confirmed exact project/CIF correspondence, exact normalized fractions, nonnegative widths/background, and no replay review flags. I’m doing the final file-level audit now.

Completed and independently replay-validated.

Crystalline phase fractions:

- WO₃, triclinic P-1 model: 72.677 wt%
- β-MnO₂, rutile: 26.372 wt%
- MnWO₄, wolframite: 0.305 wt% — tentative trace phase
- Mn₂O₃, bixbyite model: 0.647 wt% — tentative trace phase

Fit: Rwp 5.420%, Rp 4.064%, GOF 2.016. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline material. WO₃ symmetry remains pseudo-monoclinic/ triclinic ambiguous, and the exact Mn₂O₃ symmetry is not reliably resolved at its trace abundance.

